// ============================================================================
// Hunt: ScreenConnect MSP campaign - rogue remote-access clients pushed
//   through compromised MSP environments (Huntress, November 11, 2026)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1219 (Remote Access Software), T1105 (Ingress Tool
//   Transfer), T1059.003 (Windows Command Shell), T1078 (Valid Accounts)
// Data: Microsoft Defender DeviceFileEvents, DeviceProcessEvents
//   (Defender for Endpoint)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-12-2026.html
// Logic: Huntress discovered an active campaign on November 11, 2026
//   (first activity around 17:47 UTC) targeting cloud-hosted
//   ScreenConnect instances. Attackers compromise a managed service
//   provider's ScreenConnect environment, then use its Run Command
//   feature to deploy rogue remote-access clients onto customer devices
//   - the MSP's own management tool becomes the delivery mechanism. Six
//   MSPs and 286 affected endpoints across 47 organizations were
//   confirmed within hours of discovery, and Huntress says the numbers
//   are still climbing. One artifact tied to the campaign is a script
//   named SSA-2026-STATEMENT-15518787.bat, impersonating a Social
//   Security Administration statement, first uploaded to VirusTotal on
//   July 25, 2026. ConnectWise (which makes ScreenConnect) says it is
//   investigating and has urged partners to avoid interactive remote
//   sessions with potentially compromised devices. No CVE or patch
//   applies - this is abuse of legitimate remote-access features, so
//   the fix is containment, credential rotation, and hunting for rogue
//   clients, not patching. The threat actor is unnamed and how the MSP
//   environments were first compromised is not known, so no actor IoCs
//   are invented here - every query hunts the behaviour and the one
//   named artifact from the brief. Any hit on Variant 1 or 2 means a
//   device may have a rogue remote-access client installed - isolate
//   it and check whether its MSP's ScreenConnect environment has been
//   reviewed before reconnecting.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: the named campaign artifact SSA-2026-STATEMENT-15518787.bat --
// The only artifact Huntress tied to the campaign by name. It impersonates
// an SSA statement and was first seen on VirusTotal on July 25, 2026 -
// before the November MSP campaign - so the window is 30 days, not just
// the campaign week. Any execution or file creation of this name on an
// endpoint is worth triaging immediately.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName =~ "SSA-2026-STATEMENT-15518787.bat"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, SHA256, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 1b: the same artifact executed as a process --
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName =~ "SSA-2026-STATEMENT-15518787.bat"
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: ScreenConnect spawning shells, installers or downloaders --
// The campaign's delivery mechanism is ScreenConnect's own Run Command
// feature, so the signal is the ScreenConnect client/service process
// launching a child process. A remote-access agent legitimately spawns
// processes during a support session, so this variant is scoped to
// shells, installers and downloaders - the child processes that install
// a rogue client - and to the 7-day campaign window. Triage any hit
// against the MSP's session records: a Run Command with no matching
// support ticket is the abuse pattern.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("ScreenConnect.ClientService.exe", "ScreenConnect.WindowsClient.exe", "ScreenConnect.Client.exe")
| where FileName in~ ("cmd.exe", "powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "msiexec.exe", "rundll32.exe", "mshta.exe", "wscript.exe", "cscript.exe", "regsvr32.exe", "bitsadmin.exe", "curl.exe", "certutil.exe")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3: remote-access tools running on endpoints --
// The campaign's payload is a rogue remote-access client, but the brief
// does not name which tool the attackers install - so this variant lists
// executions of common third-party remote-access tools and leaves the
// triage to the environment: compare the hits against the remote-access
// tools your organisation (and your MSP) actually approves. Any tool
// outside that approved list, or a first-seen appearance in the
// campaign window, is a candidate rogue client. ScreenConnect itself is
// deliberately excluded here - it is covered by Variant 2.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where FileName in~ ("AnyDesk.exe", "TeamViewer.exe", "TeamViewer_Service.exe", "RustDesk.exe", "Splashtop Streamer.exe", "LogMeIn.exe", "GoToAssist.exe", "RemoteUtilities.exe", "rutserv.exe", "AteraAgent.exe", "DameWare.exe", "RealVNC.exe", "vncserver.exe", "TightVNC.exe", "Ammyy Admin.exe", "AA_v3.exe")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
